      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Enunciado:",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Some people believe that university students should study whatever they like. Others believe they should only be allowed to study subjects that will be useful in the future, such as science and technology. Discuss both views and give your opinion.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "(Intro) The question of what university students should be allowed to study is widely debated. While some argue that degree choices should be limited to useful fields like science and technology, I believe students should be free to choose their subject, as long as they are well informed about job prospects.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "(Body 1) Those who support limiting choices point out that economies need skilled professionals in areas such as engineering, medicine and computing. If governments fund university places, they argue, the money should go to subjects that create jobs and drive innovation. For example, several countries offer scholarships only in STEM fields in order to address shortages of qualified workers.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "(Body 2) On the other hand, advocates of free choice emphasise that motivation is essential for success. Students who study a subject they enjoy are more likely to complete their degree and perform well. Moreover, fields such as history, languages and the arts develop critical thinking and communication skills, which employers in every sector value. It is also difficult to predict which skills will be useful in twenty years.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "(Conclusion) In conclusion, although directing students towards science and technology may meet short-term economic needs, I believe that allowing free choice, supported by clear career guidance, produces more motivated graduates and a more flexible workforce.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Leia com o professor e marque: visão A · visão B · opinião (onde aparece?) · concessão."
          }
        ]
      },
